cture through which callers of CreateProcessW can supply extended process attributes, including a selected or spoofed parent process. [Data: Entities (426); Relationships (487)]\\n\\nSTARTUPINFOEX ATTRIBUTE ASSIGNMENT is described as the configuration step that adds the PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_PARENT_PROCESS attribute to the structure. This establishes the technical link between the startup structure and the fabricated parent-process identity used in PPID spoofing. [Data: Entities (431); Relationships (500)]\"},{\"summary\":\"CreateProcessA is shown as an invocation path for spoofing\",\"explanation\":\"CREATEPROCESSA INVOCATION is described as an ANSI Windows process-creation operation that uses EXTENDED_STARTUPINFO_PRESENT and a fabricated parent-process handle to create a child process with a spoofed parent. [Data: Entities (430); Relationships (499)]\\n\\nThe relationship to PPID SPOOFING indicates that process creation is not separate from the technique’s implementation; it is the operation through which the configured parent-process attribute is applied. The supplied data establishes the mechanism, but it does not provide a complete procedural sequence, code sample, or assessment of whether a particular invocation succeeded in a real environment.\"},{\"summary\":\"User-space manipulation makes stored parent-process metadata potentially unreliable\",\"explanation\":\"USER-SPACE PPID MANIPULATION is defined as setting InheritedFromUniqueProcessId or related parent-process attributes from user space, making the kernel-stored PPID potentially untrustworthy. [Data: Entities (436); Relationships (508)]\\n\\nThis relationship is significant for process-lineage analysis: a recorded parent identifier may not, by itself, establish the actual process that initiated a child. The evidence supports treating PPID as a potentially unreliable signal in this context, but it does not specify which additional telemetry or corroboration should be used.\"},{\"summary\":\"Kernel process structures and EDR access form a visibility layer\",\"explanation\":\"EPROCESS is described as a Windows kernel process object or data structure that can provide EDR with additional process information. The data also states that EDR can access kernel process information through structures such as EPROCESS when event data is insufficient. [Data: Entities (414); Relationships (468)]\\n\\nPPID is linked to EPROCESS because the parent-process identifier is stored in that structure. [Data: Entities (427); Relationships (490)] Together, these records indicate a contrast between user-space manipulation of parent-process metadata and EDR access to kernel-level process information. The records do not establish that EDR can always detect spoofing or that EPROCESS alone definitively resolves the true process lineage.\"},{\"summary\":\"Process parent-ID queries can inspect the child’s reported lineage\",\"explanation\":\"PROCESS PARENT-ID QUERY is defined as a kernel-information query through NtQueryInformationProcess that retrieves PROCESS_BASIC_INFORMATION, including the child process’s InheritedFromUniqueProcessId value. [Data: Entities (432)]\\n\\nIts relationship with PPID SPOOFING states that the spoofed PPID can be examined after creation through this query. This makes the query relevant to validating or observing the process’s reported parent identifier, while the supplied evidence also warns that user-space manipulation can make such metadata potentially untrustworthy. [Data: Relationships (501, 508)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community represents process-metadata spoofing beyond PPID alone\",\"explanation\":\"PPID SPOOFING is explicitly related to COMMAND LINE SPOOFING: both are described as Windows process-creation spoofing techniques that manipulate process metadata used by endpoint detection and response systems. The distinction provided is that PPID spoofing changes parent-process identity, whereas command-line spoofing changes command-line data. [Data: Relationshi